  "narrative": {
    "northStar": "The Pattern Sheet",
    "overview": "Mesura reads like a dressmaker's printed pattern: cool pattern paper underneath, white sheets laid on it, everything drawn in near-black ink, with a cobalt band across the top like the printed envelope a pattern ships in. Each person is a pattern piece and each measurement is a labeled mark on it. The body figure is literally a pattern piece: a tracing-paper fill, an ink cutting line, a dashed cobalt seam allowance inside it, a double-headed grainline down the center, and each girth drawn as a cobalt band with a triangular notch where its dimension line meets the piece.\n\nThe system is light, dense and drafted rather than decorated. Depth comes from paper on paper and from rules, never from glow. Figures are set narrow and firm on Archivo's width axis, the way a pattern prints its sizes; reading text stays at normal width. Meaning lives in line style: solid is now, dashed is before or alongside, a filled triangle notch is a change. Color is reserved: cobalt for action and the current reading, green, amber and red only for states.\n\nIt explicitly replaced a dark purple health-dashboard look; dark cards with neon accents are the rejected category default.",
    "keyCharacteristics": [
      "Cool pattern-paper ground with white sheets and 1.5px rules.",
      "One cobalt envelope band at the top; cobalt elsewhere only for action, the trend and the current mark.",
      "Condensed tabular numerals (wdth 72) for every figure; condensed labels (wdth 82); normal width for prose.",
      "Solid / dashed / notch line grammar shared by the body figure, charts and the week line.",
      "10px corners on everything that holds content or takes a tap; no pills."
    ],
    "rules": [
      {
        "name": "The Envelope Rule",
        "body": "Cobalt fills exactly one large area per screen: the header band. Below it, cobalt appears only as ink (lines, text, outlines) or on the one primary action and active toggles.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The State Ink Rule",
        "body": "Green, amber and red mean a state and nothing else. A change value is graphite unless it is judged against the person's goal; then it takes green or amber.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Narrow Numeral Rule",
        "body": "Every measured number is set at wdth 72, weight 720, tabular. Prose numbers stay at body width.",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Sentence-Case Label Rule",
        "body": "Labels are condensed and graphite, never uppercase-tracked. Width carries the label voice, not capitals.",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Paper On Paper Rule",
        "body": "Surfaces never cast shadows at rest. Only an overlay that truly floats (menu) gets the one soft cobalt-tinted shadow; no glows, no colored halos except the input focus ring.",
        "section": "elevation"
      }
    ],
    "dos": [
      "Do set every measured number in Archivo at wdth 72, weight 720, tabular figures.",
      "Do mark every change with the notch triangle (down for less, up for more) plus a screen-reader word, colored green or amber only when judged against the goal.",
      "Do draw \"now\" as a solid line and \"before\" or \"each reading\" as a dashed one, on the figure and in charts alike.",
      "Do keep 10px corners and 1.5px borders on every container, control and input.",
      "Do group related figures as cells of one ruled sheet rather than loose cards.",
      "Do keep every tap target at least 44px and pair every color state with a word."
    ],
    "donts": [
      "Don't return to dark health-dashboard cards with neon accents, the look this world replaced.",
      "Don't use pill shapes (999px radius) for buttons, toggles or tags.",
      "Don't fill a second large area with cobalt below the envelope band.",
      "Don't use green, amber, red or the range inks decoratively.",
      "Don't add shadows or glows to resting surfaces; the floating menu's shadow is the only one.",
      "Don't set labels in tracked uppercase."
    ]
  }
}